UNIT WISE QUIZ QUESTIONS:
UNIT–I: Basic Structure of Computers & Data Representation
1. Q: What are the five functional units of a computer?
A: Input, Output, Memory, Control Unit, ALU.
2. Q: Define bus structure.
A: A system of communication pathways that connect components (data bus, address bus, control bus).
3. Q: What is the main difference between multiprocessors and multicomputers?
A: Multiprocessors share memory; multicomputers have distributed memory.
4. Q: Convert (45)₁₀ into binary.
A: 101101₂.
5. Q: Convert (110101)₂ to decimal.
A: 53.
6. Q: Represent –15 in 8-bit signed 2’s complement.
A: 11110001.
7. Q: What is the 1’s complement of 1001101₂?
A: 0110010₂.
8. Q: Add 1011₂ + 1110₂ in binary.
A: 11001₂.
9. Q: What is the hexadecimal equivalent of (11011010)₂?
A: DA₁₆.
10. Q: Write IEEE 754 single-precision representation for +12.5.
A: Sign=0, Exponent=10000010, Mantissa=10010000000000000000000.
UNIT–II: Switching Theory & Digital Logic Circuits
   11. Q: State De Morgan’s First Law.
A: (A·B)’ = A’ + B’.
   12. Q: Which gates are universal gates?
A: NAND and NOR.
   13. Q: Simplify: A + A’B.
A: A + B.
   14. Q: Minimize F(A,B,C) = Σ(1,3,5,7).
A: F = B + C.
   15. Q: How many cells are there in a 4-variable K-map?
A: 16.
   16. Q: Expression for Half Adder sum output.
A: S = A ⊕ B.
   17. Q: Expression for Full Adder carry output.
A: C = AB + BC + AC.
   18. Q: Define Flip-Flop.
A: A bistable element that stores 1 bit of data.
   19. Q: Name two types of shift registers.
A: SISO, PISO, SIPO, PIPO (any two).
   20. Q: Function of a decoder.
A: Converts binary inputs into one active output line.
UNIT–III: Computer Arithmetic & Basic Processing Unit
      21. Q: Which algorithm is used for multiplication?
A: Booth’s Algorithm.
      22. Q: What is the difference between restoring and non-restoring division?
A: Restoring reverts remainder after unsuccessful subtraction; non-restoring avoids restoration.
      23. Q: Define instruction set.
A: The complete set of instructions a CPU can execute.
      24. Q: Name any two addressing modes.
A: Immediate, Direct, Indirect, Register, Indexed.
      25. Q: What is an opcode?
A: Part of instruction specifying the operation.
      26. Q: Write RTL for: R1 ← R1 + R2.
A: R1 ← R1 + R2.
      27. Q: What are micro-operations?
A: Basic operations on the data stored in registers.
      28. Q: List four types of micro-operations.
A: Arithmetic, Logic, Shift, Control.
      29. Q: What is control memory in microprogrammed control?
A: Memory that stores microinstructions.
      30. Q: What is the purpose of address sequencing in microprogramming?
A: Determines the order in which microinstructions are executed.
UNIT–IV: Input/Output & Memory Organization
         31. Q: Give an example of a peripheral device.
A: Printer, Keyboard, Mouse, Monitor.
         32. Q: What is DMA?
A: Direct Memory Access – allows devices to transfer data directly to memory without CPU.
         33. Q: Define interrupt.
A: A signal that temporarily halts CPU to handle an event.
         34. Q: What is synchronous vs. asynchronous data transfer?
A: Synchronous uses clock signal; asynchronous uses control signals (ready/acknowledge).
         35. Q: What is the hierarchy of memory?
A: Registers → Cache → Main Memory → Secondary Storage.
         36. Q: Difference between RAM and ROM.
A: RAM is volatile and read-write; ROM is non-volatile and read-only.
         37. Q: Define cache memory.
A: Small, high-speed memory between CPU and main memory.
         38. Q: Write any two cache mapping techniques.
A: Direct, Associative, Set-associative.
         39. Q: What is secondary storage?
A: Non-volatile storage like hard disks, SSDs.
         40. Q: Give one example of memory-mapped I/O.
A: Treating device registers as memory locations.
UNIT–V: Pipelining & RISC/CISC
            41. Q: Define pipelining.
A: Overlapping execution of instructions by dividing them into stages.
            42. Q: What is instruction hazard?
A: Hazard caused by dependency between instructions.
            43. Q: Define data hazard.
A: Occurs when instructions depend on results of previous instructions.
            44. Q: What is structural hazard?
A: Conflict over shared resources in pipeline.
            45. Q: Define RISC.
A: Reduced Instruction Set Computer – uses simple, fixed-length instructions.
            46. Q: Define CISC.
A: Complex Instruction Set Computer – supports complex instructions with variable length.
            47. Q: State one characteristic of RISC.
A: Large number of registers, load/store architecture.
            48. Q: State one characteristic of CISC.
A: Microprogrammed control, fewer registers.
            49. Q: What is superscalar operation?
A: Multiple instructions issued per clock cycle.
            50. Q: What is pipeline speedup ideally equal to?
A: Number of pipeline stages.
            51. Q: What is instruction-level parallelism (ILP)?
A: Ability to execute multiple instructions simultaneously.
            52. Q: Name two pipeline hazards.
A: Data hazard, Control hazard, Structural hazard (any two).
            53. Q: What is branch prediction?
A: Technique to guess the outcome of a branch to reduce stalls.
            54. Q: Which architecture uses fewer transistors in control unit – RISC or CISC?
A: RISC.
            55. Q: Give an example of a CISC processor.
A: Intel x86.
            56. Q: Give an example of a RISC processor.
A: ARM, MIPS.
            57. Q: What is instruction pipelining efficiency reduced by?
A: Pipeline hazards (data, control, structural).
            58. Q: Define superscalar pipeline.
A: CPU design that issues multiple instructions per cycle with parallel pipelines.
            59. Q: Why is pipeline not always linear in speedup?
A: Due to stalls, hazards, and unequal stage delays.
            60. Q: What is the main difference between RISC and CISC execution speed?
A: RISC executes instructions faster due to simplicity; CISC takes more cycles.


